gm-method (a shared synchronous GPU-readback call site consumed by two callers with DIFFERENT staleness tolerance -- an authoritative/physics-correctness caller vs a rendering/placement caller): don't apply one blanket async-or-sync policy to the whole call site. Split the CALLER contract instead: keep the original bounded-retry BLOCKING function as the default for the caller that cannot tolerate a stale/wrong value (e.g. a server-authoritative physics collider build, where every connected client's simulation derives from it), and add a NEW non-blocking, non-retried sibling function for the caller that can tolerate the value resolving some frames later (e.g. client-side rendering/placement, which a user may explicitly confirm is fine "100 frames stale" for something consumed at a distance/rate where staleness is imperceptible) -- the non-blocking variant does ONE GL round-trip and falls through to the caller's EXISTING fallback path on a miss (cheap on a hit, no busy-spin retry loop on a miss) rather than looping. This preserves determinism/correctness for the caller that needs it while removing the retry-loop's compounding cost from the caller that doesn't. Additionally, converting the underlying readback itself from a plain gl.readPixels to a PIXEL_PACK_BUFFER + fenceSync + bounded clientWaitSync spin (matching an already-proven fully-async probe pattern elsewhere in the same codebase) reduces the SYNCHRONOUS variant's own stall too, since a driver-side DMA transfer is measurably cheaper than the default readPixels-into-typed-array path on most GPUs -- both changes compound: the async caller mostly avoids paying the cost at all, and the sync caller pays a smaller cost when it does.